      "type": "rich-text",
      "blocks": {
        "heading_efVVMk": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "16. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34536182/\">Reverse Oxidative Stress</a>"
          }
        },
        "text_wm7R8K": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Oxidative stress is a state in which there is an imbalance between the levels of free radicals and antioxidants in the body. Free radicals are highly reactive molecules that can cause damage to proteins, fatty tissue, and DNA, leading to impaired cellular function and even programmed cell death (apoptosis).</p><p>Prolonged exposure to oxidative stress has been linked to a range of diseases, including diabetes, cancer, inflammatory conditions, and neurodegenerative diseases like Parkinson’s and Alzheimer’s. Oxidative stress is also believed to play a role in the aging process, including the loss of collagen and elasticity in the skin, which can lead to wrinkles.</p><p>Red light therapy has the <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34536182/\">ability to reverse oxidative stress</a> and trigger repair at the cellular level, which can help to reduce the negative effects of this process.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "heading_efVVMk",
        "text_wm7R8K"
      ],
